        {
          "id": "SP_POECILLASTRA_MACQUARIENSIS_KELLY_C_RDENAS_2019",
          "name": "Poecillastra macquariensis Kelly & Cárdenas, 2019",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Poecillastra",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "Solid stalk of sponge of unknown morphology, 15 mm in diameter, 20 mm high, expanding on the broken, upper surface, sides of stalk sculpted, attachment base contains patches of substrate (Fig. 5 A). Surface hispid and scratchy to the touch; texture firm, incompressible. Colour in preservative tan. Skeleton Stalk composed of huge swathes of contort oxeas and triaenes between which are abundant microscleres. Spicules MEGASCLERES (Fig. 5 D – E) Abundant contort to sinuous oxeas (Fig. 5 D) with slightly rounded tips, 3725 (2125 ‾ 5750) × 53 (30 ‾ 70) µm; medium-shafted triaenes (Fig. 5 E), rhabd slightly curved, tapering to a sharp tip, 852 (550 ‾ 1225) µm, clads of slightly uneven length, slightly curved downwards, 578 (450 ‾ 680) µm, overall cladome width, about 900 ‾ 1360 µm long, ranging to pseudocalthrops. Broken true oxeas are evident but unmeasurable. MICROSCLERES (Fig. 5 B – C, F – G) Microxeas (Fig. 5 B – C), straight to slightly curved, roughened, abundant, 332 (260 ‾ 420) × 7 (5 ‾ 8) µm, n = 20; plesiasters (Fig. 5 F), with 3 ‾ 5 microspined blunt-tipped rays, overall 67 (50 ‾ 100), ray length 37 (25 ‾ 60) µm, n = 10; metaster- to amphiaster- to spiraster-like streptasters (Fig. 5 G), with long, microspined rays, abundant, 19 (15 ‾ 20) µm long.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:9869012",
          "children": [],
